WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.234
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.534 --> 00:00:08.503
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.803 --> 00:00:17.956
Unit twenty-four: The Body and Technology. In this unit, you will debate the merging of human bodies and technology.

4
00:00:18.950 --> 00:00:20.172
transhumanism.

5
00:00:20.872 --> 00:00:27.376
transhumanism means the movement to enhance humans beyond natural limits through technology.

6
00:00:27.676 --> 00:00:30.604
Transhumanism imagines a future without ageing.

7
00:00:31.304 --> 00:00:34.665
Your turn. Say: transhumanism.

8
00:00:37.365 --> 00:00:38.447
transhumanism.

9
00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:40.428
cyborg.

10
00:00:41.128 --> 00:00:44.927
cyborg means a being that is partly human and partly machine.

11
00:00:45.227 --> 00:00:48.364
With his implant, he jokes that he is a cyborg.

12
00:00:49.064 --> 00:00:51.508
Your turn. Say: cyborg.

13
00:00:54.208 --> 00:00:55.209
cyborg.

14
00:00:56.217 --> 00:00:57.195
prosthetic.

15
00:00:57.895 --> 00:01:00.312
prosthetic means an artificial body part.

16
00:01:00.612 --> 00:01:03.784
Modern prosthetics can be controlled by muscle signals.

17
00:01:04.484 --> 00:01:07.566
Your turn. Say: prosthetic.

18
00:01:10.266 --> 00:01:11.279
prosthetic.

19
00:01:12.283 --> 00:01:13.668
neural implant.

20
00:01:14.368 --> 00:01:17.876
neural implant means a device placed in the brain or nerves.

21
00:01:18.176 --> 00:01:21.430
A neural implant helped a paralysed man walk again.

22
00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:25.711
Your turn. Say: neural implant.

23
00:01:28.411 --> 00:01:29.667
neural implant.

24
00:01:30.683 --> 00:01:32.451
brain-computer interface.

25
00:01:33.151 --> 00:01:37.774
brain-computer interface means a system connecting the brain directly to a computer.

26
00:01:38.074 --> 00:01:41.653
Brain-computer interfaces let patients type with their thoughts.

27
00:01:42.353 --> 00:01:46.456
Your turn. Say: brain-computer interface.

28
00:01:49.156 --> 00:01:50.958
brain-computer interface.

29
00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:53.311
human enhancement.

30
00:01:54.011 --> 00:01:58.391
human enhancement means improving human abilities beyond normal levels.

31
00:01:58.691 --> 00:02:01.758
Human enhancement raises questions of fairness.

32
00:02:02.458 --> 00:02:05.993
Your turn. Say: human enhancement.

33
00:02:08.693 --> 00:02:10.031
human enhancement.

34
00:02:11.017 --> 00:02:12.447
bodily autonomy.

35
00:02:13.147 --> 00:02:16.923
bodily autonomy means the right to control one's own body.

36
00:02:17.223 --> 00:02:20.720
Bodily autonomy is a core principle of medical ethics.

37
00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:24.549
Your turn. Say: bodily autonomy.

38
00:02:27.249 --> 00:02:28.598
bodily autonomy.

39
00:02:29.583 --> 00:02:30.759
augmentation.

40
00:02:31.459 --> 00:02:35.606
augmentation means adding to or strengthening human capacities.

41
00:02:35.906 --> 00:02:39.949
Memory augmentation may one day be possible.

42
00:02:40.649 --> 00:02:44.172
Your turn. Say: augmentation.

43
00:02:46.872 --> 00:02:48.001
augmentation.

44
00:02:49.017 --> 00:02:51.237
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:51.537 --> 00:02:56.334
Ms. Sarah, a man in my town has a prosthetic leg controlled by his muscles.

46
00:02:57.650 --> 00:03:02.276
Remarkable. Such technology was science fiction thirty years ago.

47
00:03:03.583 --> 00:03:06.129
And now there are brain-computer interfaces.

48
00:03:07.417 --> 00:03:14.855
Indeed. These devices restore abilities. The latter question, enhancement, is far more controversial.

49
00:03:16.150 --> 00:03:17.801
You mean transhumanism?

50
00:03:19.117 --> 00:03:26.088
Precisely. Its supporters see augmentation as progress; its critics, as a threat to equality.

51
00:03:27.383 --> 00:03:28.582
Who decides?

52
00:03:29.883 --> 00:03:38.329
Ultimately, bodily autonomy suggests the individual should. That principle, however, has limits in a competitive society.

53
00:03:39.617 --> 00:03:43.961
Grammar focus: Cohesion and reference (extended).

54
00:03:44.261 --> 00:03:47.933
Elegant cohesion combines reference and parallel structure.

55
00:03:48.233 --> 00:03:53.288
Such technology was science fiction. The latter question is more controversial.

56
00:03:53.588 --> 00:03:57.306
Its supporters see progress; its critics, a threat.

57
00:03:57.606 --> 00:04:02.648
Elegant cohesion combines reference words with parallel structure and ellipsis.

58
00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:06.349
Now read and listen. Beyond the Human?.

59
00:04:06.649 --> 00:04:11.609
Technology has always extended the human body, from spectacles to hearing aids.

60
00:04:12.917 --> 00:04:19.386
Today, prosthetics controlled by nerve signals and brain-computer interfaces are transforming lives.

61
00:04:20.683 --> 00:04:25.109
Such devices restore lost abilities, and few would question their value.

62
00:04:26.417 --> 00:04:30.669
Human enhancement, however, raises a different set of questions.

63
00:04:31.983 --> 00:04:38.371
Transhumanists argue that augmenting memory, strength or lifespan is a natural next step.

64
00:04:39.683 --> 00:04:43.993
Their critics fear a world divided between the enhanced and the unenhanced.

65
00:04:45.283 --> 00:04:54.818
Bodily autonomy supports individual choice, yet that principle becomes complicated when enhancement confers competitive advantage.

66
00:04:56.117 --> 00:05:02.342
The boundary between healing and enhancing may prove to be the defining ethical frontier of this century.

67
00:05:03.650 --> 00:05:06.442
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:06.742 --> 00:05:10.587
A being that is partly human and partly machine is a blank.

69
00:05:10.887 --> 00:05:16.050
A, cyborg. B, prosthetic. C, transhumanism.

70
00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:22.061
The answer is A: cyborg.

71
00:05:23.550 --> 00:05:28.057
Coffee and tea are popular; blank is more common in Cambodia.

72
00:05:28.357 --> 00:05:32.754
A, the latter. B, the after. C, the second.

73
00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:39.133
The answer is A: the latter.

74
00:05:40.617 --> 00:05:44.044
Some see opportunity; blank, danger.

75
00:05:44.344 --> 00:05:48.137
A, others. B, other. C, the others one.

76
00:05:52.150 --> 00:05:54.463
The answer is A: others.

77
00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:58.542
The right to control one's own body is blank.

78
00:05:58.842 --> 00:06:04.295
A, bodily autonomy. B, augmentation. C, human enhancement.

79
00:06:08.283 --> 00:06:11.328
The answer is A: bodily autonomy.

80
00:06:12.817 --> 00:06:17.904
According to the text, what may be the defining ethical frontier of this century?

81
00:06:18.204 --> 00:06:24.342
A, the boundary between healing and enhancing. B, space travel. C, prosthetics.

82
00:06:28.350 --> 00:06:32.021
The answer is A: the boundary between healing and enhancing.

83
00:06:33.517 --> 00:06:39.849
Now it's your turn. Part one: Should people be allowed to enhance their bodies with technology?

84
00:06:45.149 --> 00:06:51.189
Part two: Use cohesive reference to compare restoration and enhancement, then conclude.

85
00:06:57.489 --> 00:07:12.335
Restoration is widely accepted; enhancement, less so. The former helps people regain lost abilities, while the latter could create new inequalities. Such risks suggest that enhancement should be regulated, though not necessarily banned.

86
00:07:12.635 --> 00:07:15.099
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:16.083 --> 00:07:21.116
Great work today! You learned eight new words and cohesion and reference (extended).

88
00:07:21.416 --> 00:07:25.914
Today's value is curiosity. Ask questions and find out.

89
00:07:26.214 --> 00:07:29.575
See you in Unit twenty-five. Goodbye!

90
00:07:29.875 --> 00:07:32.666
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

